# Write through actions, read through selectors

## Context

`app/src/actions.ts` keeps every action payload shape in one place, and
`app/src/selectors.ts` keeps every derived read in one place (`visibleTasks`,
`remainingCount`). When a consumer builds `{ type: "task/added", payload: ... }`
inline or filters `state.tasks` at the call site, that knowledge leaks and the
next payload change breaks callers the compiler cannot find.

## Rule

- Never construct an action object literal outside `actions.ts`. Consumers
  call `dispatch(addTask(id, title))`, not `dispatch({ type: "task/added", ... })`.
- Every `Action` union variant has exactly one creator, named after the intent
  in camelCase (`addTask`, `setFilter`), returning `Action` — not a narrowed
  literal type.
- Action creators are pure mappings from arguments to payload. No ID
  generation, no defaults pulled from global state, no validation side effects.
- Derived reads live in `selectors.ts` as `(state: AppState) => T` pure
  functions. Any `state.tasks.filter/map/reduce` that appears twice outside
  this file becomes a selector.
- Selectors NEVER mutate, sort in place, or dispatch. `sort` needs a copy:
  `[...state.tasks].sort(...)`.
- Both files export types-only imports from `./types.js` — they must not
  import `store.ts` (that direction would be circular).

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "type: \"" app/src --include=*.ts` — matches only in `types.ts`,
   `actions.ts`, and test files.
2. Each variant in the `Action` union in `types.ts` has a creator in
   `actions.ts`: compare `grep -c "type: \"" app/src/actions.ts` with the
   number of union members.
3. `grep -n "store" app/src/actions.ts app/src/selectors.ts` — no import of
   `./store.js`.
4. `grep -rn "state\.tasks\.\(filter\|map\)" app/src` outside
   `selectors.ts`/`reducer.ts`/tests — zero hits.
